WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.180
How does Banker’s safety algorithm

2
00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.240
work? It asks whether at

3
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.860
least one order lets every

4
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.020
process obtain its remaining declared

5
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.620
need and eventually finish. A

6
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.880
safe state has such an

7
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.780
order; an unsafe state lacks

8
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.520
this guarantee but is not

9
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.440
automatically already deadlocked.

10
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.060
The snapshot contains Allocation, Max

11
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.300
and Available. Compute Need by

12
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.280
subtracting Allocation from Max for

13
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.940
each resource component. Keep the

14
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.180
three resource columns separate; adding

15
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.820
them into one total loses

16
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.680
the information needed for feasibility.

17
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.080
Use the lecture example with

18
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.240
five processes and initial Available

19
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.300
three, three, two. Initialize Work

20
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.340
to that vector and mark

21
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.700
every process unfinished. Work is

22
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.660
the hypothetical resource pool used

23
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.180
during the safety test, rather

24
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.680
than a permanent allocation to

25
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.600
a real running process.

26
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.080
Search for an unfinished row

27
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.560
whose Need is less than

28
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.900
or equal to Work in

29
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.140
every component. P one needs

30
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.080
one, two, two and is

31
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.320
feasible against three, three, two.

32
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.940
Equality is allowed, so a

33
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.100
row requiring exactly the available

34
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.760
count must not be rejected.

35
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.780
Pretend P one completes, then

36
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.880
add its current Allocation two,

37
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.220
zero, zero back to Work.

38
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.880
Work becomes five, three, two.

39
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.700
Release Allocation, not Max or

40
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.920
Need, because the temporary resources

41
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.720
used to finish are already

42
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.580
accounted for within the hypothetical

43
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:27.940
pool.

44
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.900
Next P three can finish

45
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.500
and release two, one, one,

46
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.620
giving Work seven, four, three.

47
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.340
P four can then finish

48
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.080
and release zero, zero, two,

49
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.140
giving seven, four, five. Mark

50
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.900
each completed process so its

51
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.620
allocation is not released twice.

52
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.840
P two now fits and

53
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.340
releases three, zero, two, producing

54
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.820
ten, four, seven. P zero

55
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.220
finally releases zero, one, zero,

56
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.060
producing ten, five, seven. Every

57
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.980
row finished, so P one,

58
00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.360
P three, P four, P

59
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.720
two, P zero is a

60
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.840
valid safe sequence.

61
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.080
A different valid sequence can

62
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.240
also prove safety. But finding

63
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.340
one infeasible candidate does not

64
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.480
prove the state unsafe; search

65
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.020
the other unfinished rows. Only

66
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.540
when no unfinished row fits

67
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.560
and some remain unfinished has

68
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.760
this repeated-search safety test become

69
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.120
stuck.

70
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.100
For an added implementation analysis,

71
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.300
at most n successful passes

72
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.620
can finish n processes, and

73
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.260
each pass can inspect n

74
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.080
rows of m resource components.

75
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.320
A straightforward repeated-search implementation therefore

76
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.360
takes order n squared times

77
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.100
m work. This is a

78
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.820
derived bound, not an additional

79
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.500
claim about examination scope.

80
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.240
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.380
Need equals Max minus Allocation,

82
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.460
feasibility is componentwise less than

83
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.660
or equal, and completion releases

84
00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.200
Allocation. Keep Work, Finish and

85
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.860
the proposed sequence visible so

86
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.340
every update can be checked.
